---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import FAQSection from "../components/FAQSection.astro";

const carFaqs = [
  {
    question: "How much car can I realistically afford?",
    answer:
      "A realistic car budget should fit comfortably after housing, debt payments, savings, insurance, utilities, groceries, and normal monthly expenses are covered.",
  },
  {
    question: "Should I budget based on the monthly payment or total cost?",
    answer:
      "The total ownership cost matters more than the payment alone. Insurance, fuel, maintenance, interest, registration, depreciation, and repairs all affect affordability.",
  },
  {
    question: "How much of my income should go toward a car?",
    answer:
      "Many households try to keep total vehicle costs manageable relative to take-home income so savings and emergency flexibility are not squeezed.",
  },
  {
    question: "Does loan length matter?",
    answer:
      "Yes. Longer loans reduce the monthly payment but can increase total interest costs and negative-equity risk.",
  },
  {
    question: "Is it smarter to buy a cheaper car?",
    answer:
      "Often, yes. A cheaper vehicle can create more room for savings, emergencies, investing, travel, and lower financial stress overall.",
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="How Much Car Can I Afford?"
  description="Learn how to calculate a realistic car budget using income, debt, savings, insurance, loan length, and total ownership cost."
>
  <style>
    .container {
      max-width: 920px;
      margin: 0 auto;
      padding: 80px 24px;
    }

    .eyebrow {
      color: #0f766e;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: 2px;
      font-size: 0.8rem;
      font-weight: 800;
      margin-bottom: 18px;
    }

    h1 {
      font-size: clamp(3rem, 7vw, 5rem);
      line-height: 0.95;
      margin-bottom: 28px;
      color: #0f172a;
      max-width: 860px;
    }

    .intro {
      font-size: 1.12rem;
      line-height: 1.9;
      color: #334155;
      max-width: 820px;
      margin-bottom: 60px;
    }

    .content-section {
      margin-top: 72px;
    }

    .content-section h2 {
      font-size: 2rem;
      line-height: 1.2;
      margin-bottom: 24px;
      color: #0f172a;
    }

    .content-section h3 {
      font-size: 1.3rem;
      margin-top: 30px;
      margin-bottom: 12px;
      color: #0f172a;
    }

    .content-section p,
    .content-section li {
      color: #334155;
      line-height: 1.9;
      font-size: 1.06rem;
      margin-bottom: 22px;
    }

    .callout {
      background: #f8fafc;
      border: 1px solid #dbe4ee;
      border-radius: 22px;
      padding: 26px;
      margin-top: 32px;
    }

    .callout p {
      margin: 0;
      font-size: 1.08rem;
    }

    .link-grid {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr));
      gap: 20px;
      margin-top: 28px;
    }

    .link-card {
      display: block;
      background: white;
      border: 1px solid #dbe4ee;
      border-radius: 18px;
      padding: 22px;
      text-decoration: none;
      color: inherit;
      transition: transform 0.15s ease;
    }

    .link-card:hover {
      transform: translateY(-2px);
    }

    .link-card strong {
      display: block;
      margin-bottom: 10px;
      color: #0f172a;
      line-height: 1.3;
    }

    .link-card span {
      color: #334155;
      line-height: 1.7;
      font-size: 0.96rem;
    }

    .info-grid {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr));
      gap: 22px;
      margin-top: 28px;
    }

    .info-card {
      background: white;
      border: 1px solid #dbe4ee;
      border-radius: 18px;
      padding: 24px;
    }

    .info-card h3 {
      margin-top: 0;
    }

    .info-card p {
      margin-bottom: 0;
      font-size: 0.98rem;
    }
  </style>

  <main class="container">
    <div class="eyebrow">Car Affordability Guide</div>

    <h1>How Much Car Can I Afford?</h1>

    <p class="intro">
      A car payment is only part of the real ownership cost. Insurance, fuel,
      maintenance, repairs, loan interest, depreciation, and monthly debt
      obligations all affect whether a vehicle actually fits your financial
      life. This guide explains how to build a realistic car budget without
      sacrificing savings, flexibility, or long-term stability.
    </p>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Start With Your Full Monthly Budget</h2>

      <p>
        Many buyers focus on the monthly payment because that is how vehicles
        are marketed. But lenders approving a loan does not automatically mean
        the payment fits comfortably into your broader financial picture.
      </p>

      <p>
        Before deciding how much car you can afford, evaluate:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>Take-home income</li>
        <li>Housing costs</li>
        <li>Debt payments</li>
        <li>Emergency savings</li>
        <li>Insurance costs</li>
        <li>Fuel and transportation expenses</li>
        <li>Childcare or family costs</li>
        <li>Retirement contributions</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        A vehicle should fit within the budget that exists after normal life
        expenses are already covered.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>The True Cost of Car Ownership</h2>

      <p>
        The monthly loan payment is often only part of the expense. Total
        ownership costs can become much larger once insurance, fuel,
        maintenance, tires, registration, taxes, parking, and depreciation are
        included.
      </p>

      <div class="info-grid">
        <div class="info-card">
          <h3>Insurance</h3>

          <p>
            Luxury vehicles, trucks, sports cars, and financed vehicles often
            carry much higher premiums than expected.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="info-card">
          <h3>Maintenance</h3>

          <p>
            Tires, brakes, oil changes, repairs, batteries, and routine service
            can significantly raise the long-term ownership cost.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="info-card">
          <h3>Depreciation</h3>

          <p>
            Many vehicles lose value quickly, especially newer vehicles during
            the first few years of ownership.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="info-card">
          <h3>Interest</h3>

          <p>
            Longer loans and higher interest rates can increase the real cost of
            the vehicle far beyond the sticker price.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Why Loan Length Matters</h2>

      <p>
        Longer loans can make expensive vehicles appear affordable because they
        reduce the monthly payment. But stretching a loan over six, seven, or
        eight years usually increases total interest and keeps the borrower in
        debt longer.
      </p>

      <p>
        Longer loans also increase the risk of negative equity, where the loan
        balance stays higher than the vehicle's value for an extended period.
      </p>

      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          A lower monthly payment does not automatically mean a vehicle is more
          affordable.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>When a More Expensive Vehicle Can Make Sense</h2>

      <p>
        Higher vehicle costs can make sense when the purchase supports work,
        family logistics, safety, reliability, or major transportation needs  - 
        and the rest of the financial picture remains stable afterward.
      </p>

      <p>
        A stronger income, low debt load, large emergency fund, and healthy
        retirement savings can make higher vehicle costs more manageable.
      </p>

      <p>
        The danger appears when a vehicle payment leaves little breathing room
        for emergencies, savings, travel, housing, or normal monthly surprises.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>When You Should Probably Spend Less</h2>

      <p>
        A cheaper vehicle is usually the smarter move if:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>The payment depends on a very long loan</li>
        <li>You are carrying high-interest debt</li>
        <li>Emergency savings are weak</li>
        <li>The payment crowds out retirement savings</li>
        <li>You would struggle with repairs or insurance increases</li>
        <li>The vehicle is being purchased mostly for lifestyle signaling</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Lower vehicle costs often create more flexibility for future goals,
        including travel, home ownership, investing, family expenses, and lower
        financial stress.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="FinancialRedFlags" class="content-section">
      <h2>Car Affordability Red Flags</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>The payment only works with a six-, seven-, or eight-year loan.</li>
        <li>You are ignoring insurance, repairs, fuel, tires, registration, taxes, or depreciation.</li>
        <li>The vehicle payment would slow emergency savings, debt payoff, or retirement contributions.</li>
        <li>You would struggle if insurance, repairs, or interest costs were higher than expected.</li>
        <li>The car is being justified mainly by monthly payment instead of total ownership cost.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="ReduceCostTips" class="content-section">
      <h2>Ways to Lower Car Costs</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Shop based on total ownership cost, not only the monthly payment.</li>
        <li>Compare insurance quotes before choosing the vehicle.</li>
        <li>Consider a less expensive trim, used vehicle, or shorter loan term.</li>
        <li>Increase the down payment if it does not drain emergency savings.</li>
        <li>Avoid add-ons, warranties, and dealer extras that raise the financed amount.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="KeyCostCategories" class="content-section">
      <h2>Key Car Cost Categories</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Monthly loan or lease payment.</li>
        <li>Insurance premiums, deductibles, and coverage requirements.</li>
        <li>Fuel, charging, parking, tolls, registration, taxes, and inspection fees.</li>
        <li>Maintenance, tires, brakes, repairs, batteries, and routine service.</li>
        <li>Loan interest, depreciation, negative-equity risk, and resale value.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="CalculatorAssumptions" class="content-section">
      <h2>Car Budget Assumptions</h2>
      <p>
        This guide assumes car affordability should be judged using take-home income,
        existing debt, emergency savings, insurance, total ownership cost, and loan
        length rather than the monthly payment alone.
      </p>
      <p>
        A car is treated as safer when the payment and ownership costs leave room for
        housing, groceries, utilities, savings, repairs, debt payoff, and normal monthly
        surprises.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="MethodologySection" class="content-section">
      <h2>How ShouldISpend Evaluates Car Affordability</h2>
      <p>
        ShouldISpend evaluates car affordability as a pressure decision. A lender may
        approve a loan, but that does not mean the vehicle fits comfortably inside the
        household budget.
      </p>
      <p>
        The strongest warning signs are long loan terms, weak emergency savings,
        high existing debt, ignored ownership costs, and choosing a vehicle that leaves
        too little breathing room after normal expenses.
      </p>
    </section>


    <section id="RelatedCalculators" class="content-section">
      <h2>Helpful Car Affordability Tools</h2>

      <div class="link-grid">
        <a class="link-card" href="/should-i-spend-700-on-a-car-payment/">
          <strong>$700 Car Payment Calculator</strong>

          <span>
            Evaluate whether a $700 monthly payment fits your broader financial
            picture.
          </span>
        </a>

        <a class="link-card" href="/should-i-spend-1000-on-a-car-payment/">
          <strong>$1,000 Car Payment Calculator</strong>

          <span>
            Test whether a luxury-level payment creates too much financial
            pressure.
          </span>
        </a>

        <a class="link-card" href="/what-counts-as-debt/">
          <strong>What Counts as Debt?</strong>

          <span>
            Understand which obligations should be included when evaluating car
            affordability.
          </span>
        </a>

        <a class="link-card" href="/how-much-emergency-savings-should-i-have/">
          <strong>Emergency Savings Guide</strong>

          <span>
            Learn how much cushion to keep before taking on a large monthly
            vehicle payment.
          </span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <FAQSection title="Car Affordability FAQ" faqs={carFaqs} />
  </main>
</Layout>